From d1625d1b384a5ea5ec044be7a53d334c6745394f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 17:30:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 09/14] mmu: fix flush_cacheable_pages off-by-one touching
 guard page

flush_cacheable_pages() accumulates contiguous cacheable page ranges and
tracks flush_end as the exclusive end of each range: the address of the
first page BEYOND the last cacheable block, which equals the start
address of the next block. The flush_end == addr extension test relies
on this invariant holding everywhere flush_end is assigned.

Two places break the invariant.

First: when a non-cacheable page (e.g. the stack guard page) creates a
gap in the middle of a flush region followed by more cacheable pages,
dma_flush_range_end(flush_start, flush_end) is called just before
starting a new range. dma_flush_range_end() treats its second argument
as an inclusive end and calls v8_flush_dcache_range(start, end + 1), so
the exclusive flush_end becomes an exclusive bound of end+1. This causes
dc civac to execute at flush_end itself: the first address of the gap.
On ARMv8 (Cortex-A55/A76), dc civac requires write permission to the
VA. When flush_end equals the guard-page address (MAP_FAULT, no
access), the instruction raises a synchronous Data Abort that barebox's
fault handler reports as "stack overflow" because FAR matches the guard
page.

Second: flush_end is clamped against region_end via
min(flush_end + block_size, region_end), in both the range-extension
branch and right after starting a new range. region_end is computed as
PAGE_ALIGN(region_start + size) - 1, an inclusive last-address value.
Because block_size is always a multiple of PAGE_SIZE (4K/2M/1G
translation granules) and region_end + 1 is always page-aligned, this
clamp can only ever land exactly on region_end when a cacheable run
reaches the end of the requested region -- the ordinary case for most
flush calls. When that happens, flush_end silently holds an inclusive
value instead of the exclusive one it holds everywhere else.

Fix both by keeping flush_end consistently exclusive: clamp against
region_end + 1 (not region_end) at both extension sites, and subtract
1 to convert to the inclusive end dma_flush_range_end expects at both
call sites. With flush_end always exclusive, both call sites can apply
the same -1 conversion unconditionally and get a correct inclusive end
regardless of whether flush_end came from range extension or from
hitting the region boundary.

Observed on RK3588S (Radxa CM5) during NVMe/PCIe bring-up.

Signed-off-by: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
---
 arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h | 21 ++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h b/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h
index a03e10810d..14cb0b431a 100644
--- a/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h
+++ b/arch/arm/cpu/flush_cacheable_pages.h
@@ -54,24 +54,35 @@ static void flush_cacheable_pages(void *start, size_t size)
 			/*
 			 * While it's safe to flush the whole block_size,
 			 * it's unnecessary time waste to go beyond region_end.
+			 * Clamp against region_end + 1, not region_end: flush_end
+			 * is exclusive everywhere in this function (that's what
+			 * the flush_end == addr check above relies on), but
+			 * region_end is an inclusive last-address value. Clamping
+			 * straight to region_end would silently make flush_end
+			 * inclusive in this one case, and the dma_flush_range_end()
+			 * call below always treats flush_end as exclusive.
 			 */
-			flush_end = min(flush_end + block_size, region_end);
+			flush_end = min(flush_end + block_size, region_end + 1);
 			continue;
 		}

 		/*
 		 * We don't have a previous contiguous flush area to append to.
-		 * If we recorded any area before, let's flush it now
+		 * If we recorded any area before, let's flush it now.
+		 * flush_end is the exclusive end of the cacheable range. Subtract
+		 * 1 to convert to the inclusive end expected by dma_flush_range_end,
+		 * preventing dc civac from stepping into the next (possibly fault-mapped)
+		 * page when a non-cacheable page (e.g. the stack guard) follows.
 		 */
 		if (flush_start != ~0UL)
-			dma_flush_range_end(flush_start, flush_end);
+			dma_flush_range_end(flush_start, flush_end - 1);

 		/* and start the new contiguous flush area with this page */
 		flush_start = addr;
-		flush_end = min(flush_start + block_size, region_end);
+		flush_end = min(flush_start + block_size, region_end + 1);
 	}

 	/* The previous loop won't flush the last cached range, so do it here */
 	if (flush_start != ~0UL)
-		dma_flush_range_end(flush_start, flush_end);
+		dma_flush_range_end(flush_start, flush_end - 1);
 }
